---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { withBase } from "../lib/base";
---

<Base
  title="The Floor"
  description="A free tool that turns a few clicks into a generic small-business website, on purpose. See what you get for nothing, then judge any quote against it."
>
  <section class="wrap hero-land">
    <h1>A free website generator. On purpose, it's nothing special.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Small businesses pay thousands for websites that took one template. Build the template yourself in five minutes, then judge any quote against
      it.
    </p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn btn--tape btn--big" href={withBase("build/")}>Build the baseline</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--big" href={withBase("red-flags/")}>Red flags when hiring</a>
    </div>
    <p class="privacy-line">No account. No AI. Nothing you enter leaves your browser.</p>
  </section>
  <hr class="floor wrap" style="margin-top: 2.5rem" />

  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="compare">
      <div>
        <h2>What you get for nothing</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>A tidy one-to-five-page site: home, about, services, gallery, contact.</li>
          <li>Your name, logo, colours and words dropped into a stock layout.</li>
          <li>It works on phones. Every template does now.</li>
          <li>A ZIP you can double-click to open, or drag onto a free host.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>What it's missing</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>A contact form that sends anything.</li>
          <li>Anything that helps people find you on Google.</li>
          <li>Booking, payments, menus, or anything specific to your business.</li>
          <li>Someone who asked what the site is for.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem">
          That second list is what you pay a developer for. If a quote doesn't cover it, you're paying for the first list.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" style="padding-top: 0">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Answer a few questions</strong>
        Your name, what you do, and a layout, colours and fonts from a short list. Every step can be skipped.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>See it, then see what's missing</strong>
        A live preview, and a Reality Check that lists what a real developer should add and what to ask them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Download the ZIP</strong>
        Plain HTML and CSS, the same thing a template site gives you. Keep it, host it, or use it to haggle.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" style="padding-top: 0">
    <hr class="floor floor--thin" />
    <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem"><strong>Why this exists.</strong></p>
    <p>
      People who don't build websites have nothing to compare a quote against. This gives them the comparison. It also sets a bar for the people
      who do: if you want to call yourself a developer, you have to do better than what's given away here for free.
    </p>
    <p><a href={withBase("for-developers/")}>Are you a developer? Link to this.</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
